{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789246257,"nonce":"Ffz6aNPiVwyjnBOYVpKX/j0y7fQ=","title":"LLVM top tier behind the llvm feature, promoted per function the way Ash does it","message":"Tier ladder: interpreter -\u003e Cranelift (baseline, jit_threshold) -\u003e LLVM (opt_threshold). LLVM 21 through inkwell 0.8 (llvm21-1-prefer-static), the pin recorded in 3ffe366. Cargo feature `llvm`, off by default; WLIFT_TIER1=off|cranelift|llvm picks the top tier at run time (llvm when the feature is built, cranelift otherwise).\n\nPromotion, mirroring beadie's TieredAdapter / Ash's tiering.rs without migrating the engine to the adapter (its compile closure has to run the whole compile on the broker thread from a shared context, and the engine's compile prep reads engine state):\n\n- a function is proposed for the top tier only once its baseline is installed and no compile is in flight (tier_states == BaselineNative, compiling_tier none, no optimised executable) -- the \"already completed in cranelift\" check;\n- the threshold is opt_threshold with queue-ahead of opt_threshold/5, compared with \u003e= against the bead's invocation count, guarded by a per-function retry horizon: a compile that declines re-proposes at double the count at which it declined (PromotionBroker::process), a compile that fails never re-proposes;\n- worth gate (Ash llvm/air.rs LlvmCeiling) computed on the function's MIR: no loop and at most 8 non-call instructions -\u003e None (never promoted, remembered); a loop whose body is mostly calls (calls*3 \u003e= instrs) -\u003e Low; otherwise High. Only None is refused; the decision is memoised per function. WLIFT_PROMOTE_GATE=0 disables it. Ash's demand gate (own hot loop / live frame / called from a loop) has no equivalent signal once a function is in native code here; not ported;\n- compiles run on one promoter thread with a bounded queue, not a thread per function.\n\nOSR between the tiers (Ash retier.rs, cranelift/air.rs retier_worth_polling): baseline code polls a per-function word at its OUTERMOST loop headers only (leaf-loop polls were ruinous in Ash). When the LLVM body installs with OSR entries the word is set; the poll's slow path boxes the header's live-ins in the bytecode's register numbering, the same (register, value) buffer the cold-loop exit uses, and calls wren_retier, which finds the LLVM OSR entry for that header, orders the values by the entry's live_in_regs, checks live_in_num/live_in_int, and runs the entry to completion; its result is the baseline frame's return value. A declined transfer clears the word so the poll stops paying for the helper call.\n\nLLVM lowering: same MIR the optimised Cranelift tier takes (guards from the profile, unbox-params, int specialisation, inlined known calls), same wren_* runtime ABI (all-i64 helpers reached through constant addresses), block parameters as allocas for mem2reg, frame-pointer=all, default\u003cO2\u003e, MCJIT. OSR entry variants per loop header as in the Cranelift backend. Not lowered inline in the first cut: SIMD intrinsics, typed-array subscripts, the f64 inner-function variant for self-recursive numeric functions -- those take the generic helper path.","files":null}]}